  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nFull sun returns today as high pressure passes to the north.\nTemperatures are set to be a couple degrees warmer under the\nsun. NW flow will prevail in the morning, but may not be strong\nenough to hold an afternoon seabreeze at bay. This will likely\nwork inland during the mid to late afternoon.\n\nThe high will tend to linger over northern New England into the\nevening and overnight hours, but moisture will be just to the\nsouthwest along a stationary front. While northward progression\nof precipitation is expected as the high's influence weakens,\nrain may only make it into southern NH shortly after midnight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nFast WNW 500 mb flow overspreads the Northeast U.S., on the \nperiphery of a strong ridge to our south. It will be a favorable \nsetup for disturbances to ride the ridge and bring some rain chances \nto New England. With that said the heavy rain signal has been \nsagging south of the forecast in recent runs, alongside the warm \nfront and meaningful instability.\n\nTuesday will be an overcast day outside of possibly far northern \nMaine, with rain chances increasing with distance southwest. Could \nbe some embedded downpours and rumbles of thunder in southwest \nNH during the morning with a bit of elevated instability. Much \ncooler with onshore flow keeping temps in the 60s north of the \nfront, but milder lows in the 50s Tuesday night. SW NH might get\nclose to the warm front, but for now the majority of guidance \nindicates it stays to the south.\n\nShould see a brief break in the action with some subsidence Tuesday \nnight-Wed morning before the next shortwave in the flow moves \nthrough Wed afternoon-Thursday. Generally looks like a rinse and \nrepeat of the first wave in terms of track and impacts; cool temps \nnorth of the front, and the highest chance for heavier rains towards \nSW NH. It remains to be seen if we see a separate round of rain\nlater on Thursday with the front, but generally expect \nimproving conditions towards Thursday afternoon/night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nWave that moves through here Thursday closes off near Nova Scotia, \nbringing cyclonic northerly flow to the region. This should result \nin falling dew points and near seasonable to slightly cool \ntemperatures. Cannot rule out isolated showers Friday-Saturday with \nrather cold temperatures aloft as a potential trigger, but PoPs drop \nfrom 50-80% Thursday to 15-30% for the following two days. Finally, \ncould be quite breezy near the shoreline if a stronger coastal low \ncan manifest.",
